# Response headers for the static web export, read by Cloudflare Workers Static
# Assets (`wrangler.toml` → `[assets]`). `expo export --platform web` copies
# `public/` into `dist/` verbatim, so this file ships as `dist/_headers` and is
# never referenced by the app itself.
#
# THE `/*` RULE AT THE BOTTOM IS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE, and it exists because of
# a platform difference that no build, deploy or test in this repository can
# see. Cloudflare PAGES adds `x-content-type-options: nosniff` and
# `referrer-policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin` to every response it
# serves, automatically, with nothing in the project asking for them — measured
# fleet-wide on `oxy-auth.pages.dev`, a Pages project carrying no `_headers`
# rule for either. Workers Static Assets does NOT. GoWay was born on Workers,
# so it never had them; without this rule it never would, and nothing here
# reads a response header to notice. `nosniff` is the one that matters: without
# it a browser may disregard a declared Content-Type and sniff the bytes
# instead, which turns any response carrying attacker-influenced content into a
# possible script.

# Expo emits content-hashed JS chunks under /_expo/static and every imported
# asset — Bloom's .woff2 web fonts among them — under /assets. Both carry a
# content hash in the filename, so a changed file is a changed URL and the
# response can be cached forever.
/_expo/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The MapLibre GL worker and shared chunk, copied out of the INSTALLED
# maplibre-gl by scripts/vendor-maplibre-worker.js into a path keyed on the
# package version (`/vendor/maplibre-gl/<version>/…`). Version-keyed is the
# same guarantee a content hash gives: a bump changes the URL, so the old URL
# may be cached indefinitely.
/vendor/maplibre-gl/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# GoWay's own cartography: the style documents, the glyph ranges and the sprite
# atlas, all committed under `public/map/` and carried into `dist/` by the
# export. (`/map/tiles/*` is NOT here — it is the one map path served by the
# Worker rather than by the asset pipeline, so `worker/index.js` sets its own
# headers. Change one and look at the other.)
#
# ## Why these carry `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`
#
# Because without it GoWay is not a map platform, it is a map app. Measured
# before this rule existed:
#
#   curl -H 'Origin: https://homiio.com' https://goway.to/map/goway-light.json
#     → 200, and NO access-control-allow-origin
#
# A 200 with no ACAO is the worst shape a cross-origin fetch can have: the
# server did the work, the bytes crossed the wire, and the browser threw them
# away and logged a CORS error. Every third-party site that tried to render
# GoWay's cartography saw an empty map. The Homiio migration hit exactly this
# and correctly refused to proxy around it, because a proxy is the layer that
# migration exists to remove.
#
# The wildcard is the RIGHT answer here rather than a shortcut, and the test is
# whether the response carries anything an attacker could not fetch themselves:
#
#   - These are public cartography assets. No cookie, no token, no per-user
#     content, nothing that varies by caller. `*` can leak nothing because
#     there is nothing behind an origin check to leak.
#   - No credentials travel with them, and `*` cannot be combined with
#     credentials anyway — the spec forbids the pairing and browsers enforce
#     it, which is the property that makes a wildcard safe here and unsafe on
#     the API's credentialed routes (see `packages/backend/src/middleware/cors.ts`).
#   - It matches what the tile service our own style depends on already does:
#     `https://tiles.openfreemap.org/planet` answers
#     `access-control-allow-origin: *`. A public map service that does not is
#     not usable by anyone.
#
# `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin` is the second half of the same
# statement and is easy to forget: a `*` ACAO admits the fetch, but an embedder
# that has turned on cross-origin isolation (`COEP: require-corp` — which any
# site using SharedArrayBuffer or precise timers must) has its browser block
# every subresource that does not opt in explicitly. Without CORP those sites
# get a blocked font and a map with no labels, and the console message names
# COEP rather than GoWay.
/map/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate

# The style documents keep the five-minute rule above: these paths are NOT
# content-hashed — `provider.ts` names them literally, and a native binary in
# the field reads them from https://goway.to — so they must never be cached
# immutably. Five minutes is long enough that a map load does not re-fetch the
# document on every navigation, and short enough that a cartography deploy
# reaches every client without a version bump nobody can perform.
#
# Glyphs and the sprite are the same kind of path — literal, not hashed — but
# not the same kind of content, so they get their own rule. The style document
# is what a cartography change edits, and it is 100 kB; the glyph ranges are
# megabytes and change only when the FONT changes, which is a deliberate act
# nobody performs by accident. Re-fetching megabytes of SDF every five minutes
# to discover that Inter has not moved is a cost paid on every session for a
# change that happens once a year. A day is the compromise: a font regeneration
# reaches every client within a day without a version bump, and a normal
# session pays for the glyphs once.
#
# These rules come AFTER `/map/*` deliberately. Cloudflare applies every
# matching rule and the later one wins for a header it repeats, so each of
# these restates only `Cache-Control` and inherits the CORS pair above.
/map/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/map/sprites/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# Every response. See the note at the top of this file — these two are the
# headers Pages used to add for free and Workers does not.
#
# Deliberately NOT here: a framing header. Workers Static Assets sends neither
# `X-Frame-Options` nor a `frame-ancestors` CSP today, so `/frame` (the embed
# route — `app/frame.tsx`) is framable with no rule, which is what an embed
# route needs. Adding a global `frame-ancestors 'none'` and then carving
# `/frame` back out was considered and rejected: `/frame` and `/` are the SAME
# document under `not_found_handling = "single-page-application"`, so an
# exception for one path is an exception for the whole SPA the moment the
# router moves, and the carve-out would rest on Cloudflare's rule-precedence
# semantics for a repeated header, which nothing in this repository can test.
# The honest position is the one recorded in `app/frame.tsx`: the embed renders
# no sign-in affordance and nothing an embedder can aim a user's click at.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
